Russia's space agency says it will  launch an urgent Mission next month to  replace a damaged Soleus spacecraft  docked at the International Space  Station video shows the capsule is  leaking so officials say three crew  members including an American astronaut  need to need a ride back to Earth Mark  Strassman reports on the plan to bring  them home  and liftoff when the Russian soyuz  launched last September it ferried a  three-man crew to the International  Space Station for a six-month stay but  in December while duck to the station  their ride home suddenly sprung a leak  Russian flight controllers and the  flight controllers here at Mission  Control in Houston have been noticing a  stream of particles coming out of the  soyuz ms-22 vehicle investigators  believe a micro meteoroid a tiny Space  Rock just one millimeter across but  flying 15 000 miles per hour struck a  coolant line that feeds the spacecraft's  radiator and without that coolant  temperatures inside the cabin could  climb above 100 degrees during a return  flight potentially overheating flight  computers  Russian space officials determined the  damage craft is unsafe to fly  essentially stranding its crew Cosmonaut  sergeyev and Dimitri pedalin and NASA  astronaut Frank Rubio to bring the crew  home Russia is fast tracking a  replacement ship to launch in February  this has certainly never happened before  in the history of the space station CBS  News space analyst Bill Harwood they  don't have spare soyuz it's just sitting  in a hangar ready to go on a moment's  notice so they're taking the one for the  next crew they're going to launch the  new soyuz with no one on board and then  the crew that's currently on board the  station they're now going to have to  stay up as if they were another new crew  staying up for six months which is going  to push their flight out to nearly  nearly a year or longer a reminder that  in space even the smallest of threats  carry big risks for CBS mornings I'm  Mark Strassman
 

